    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.26599/ecm.2026.9400031",
    "abstract": "IMED|HEL is a health and economy assessment module within the Integrated Model of Energy, Environment, and Economy for Sustainable Development (IMED). It is designed to evaluate the health and economic implications of environmental and climate policy choices. The module follows a single chain from scenario assumptions to emissions, pollutant concentrations, population exposure, health outcomes, and economic metrics. It represents both ambient exposure and indoor exposure related to household fuel use. Across China-focused applications at national, regional, city, and sector scales, the framework has expanded from PM<sub>2.5</sub>-focused burden accounting to ozone-inclusive and joint PM<sub>2.5</sub>-O<sub>3</sub> assessments. It has also moved from damage estimation to evaluating end-of-pipe controls, structural transitions, and climate and air quality synergies. The model provides strong support for incorporating health damages as a core policy outcome when comparing and prioritizing pollution control and mitigation options.",
    "field": "Integrated assessment of health and environmental policy",
    "problem": "Existing climate policy assessment models often treat the health and economic impacts of air pollution simplistically or exogenously, limiting their ability to accurately quantify co-benefits and support integrated, synergistic decision-making for environmental and health protection.",
    "method": "The paper proposes the IMED|HEL model, an integrated assessment module that evaluates health and economic co-benefits of environmental policies by quantifying the full chain from policy-driven emissions and concentrations to population exposure, health burdens, and monetized economic outcomes.",
    "task": "The task is to evaluate the health and economic co-benefits of air pollution control and climate policies by quantifying the chain of impacts from emissions to health burdens and economic costs using the IMED|HEL model.",
